  Working mode: PARTNER
  Reason: autonomous replan violated constraints — escalating to manager

  [Escalation] Following a 20% demand surge, the two-cell factory schedule has been rejected due to two simultaneous constraint violations: (1) Operator H1's fatigue index has reached 0.8261, exceeding the 0.40 hard limit by 106.5% (margin: -0.4261); and (2) combined factory noise across both cells has reached 87.2 dB, exceeding the 82.0 dB ceiling by 5.2 dB (margin: -5.2). Operator H2 retains full fatigue headroom (remaining margin: +0.35), and Operator H3 is certification-constrained to inspection tasks only. The system cannot satisfy the elevated demand within current human-centric and safety constraints without managerial intervention.

  [Escalation] 5 remediation options:
    - Option 1 (Recommended): Cross-Cell Operator and Variant Redistribution: Redistribute H1's excess tasks to H2, who retains full fatigue headroom (margin: +0.35, current utilization effectively near zero). Reroute the highest-noise and most H1-intensive product variants from Cell_1 to Cell_2 within AGV capacity limits. Consolidate all inspection tasks across both cells under H3, freeing H1 and H2 for direct production duties. This simultaneously attacks both violations: H1 fatigue drops below 0.40 as tasks are offloaded, and Cell_1 noise decreases as high-noise processes migrate or are staggered. Minor throughput loss expected only from AGV inter-cell transfer overhead and scheduling resequencing.
      Preserves human constraints: Yes | Gap: ~5.0%
    - Option 2: Deploy Backup Operator to Offload H1: Introduce a certified backup operator (H4) for this shift to absorb H1's excess workload. H1 is immediately capped at tasks that hold its fatigue index at or below 0.40; all overflow is assigned to H4. To resolve the noise violation in parallel, stagger the peak-noise processes of Cell_1 and Cell_2 so they do not overlap temporally, reducing combined instantaneous noise by an estimated 5-6 dB. This option maintains near-full throughput, fully preserves all defined human-centric fatigue constraints, and requires no product deferral. Lead time to deploy H4 and adjust the schedule is the primary operational risk.
      Preserves human constraints: Yes | Gap: ~8.0%
    - Option 3: Product Mix Adjustment — Defer Highest-Impact Variant to Next Shift: Identify and remove from this shift the single product variant that contributes most to both H1 fatigue accumulation and combined factory noise. Deferring this variant to the next shift or an overflow production window selectively reduces load without penalising all SKUs. Target selection criteria: highest combined score on (H1 task-time × noise contribution). Estimated effect: H1 fatigue index returns below 0.40, factory noise drops toward 82 dB. Only the deferred SKU incurs a deadline gap; all other variants are fulfilled on schedule. This requires production planning alignment to reschedule the deferred variant promptly.
      Preserves human constraints: Yes | Gap: ~15.0%
    - Option 4: Throughput Reduction — Cap Output at Pre-Surge Demand Level: Cap total factory output for this shift at the original demand level, absorbing the full 20% demand increase as an accepted deadline gap. Proportionally scaling back task assignments returns H1 fatigue to approximately 0.69 × 0.8261 ≈ 0.57, which still violates the limit alone. A full reset to pre-surge demand levels (approximately 83% of current planned volume) brings H1 fatigue to ~0.69 and noise to ~84.5 dB — still slightly above thresholds. Throughput must be reduced to approximately 75% of current planned volume to fully resolve both violations simultaneously. The 25% unmet demand is queued for recovery across subsequent shifts. This is the operationally simplest option and the highest-risk for customer delivery commitments.
      Preserves human constraints: Yes | Gap: ~20.0%
    - Option 5 (Last Resort): Temporary Constraint Relaxation with Mandatory Monitoring Protocol: Request explicit manager authorisation to temporarily raise H1's fatigue limit to 0.85 for this shift only and raise the noise ceiling to 88.0 dB. This deviation must be conditioned on: mandatory 15-minute rest breaks for H1 every 45 minutes, full noise-exposure PPE enforcement for all operators on the floor, real-time physiological monitoring of H1, and documented management sign-off with regulatory awareness. This option preserves full throughput and closes the deadline gap but explicitly violates the human-centric and acoustic safety constraints as defined in the CBPA framework. It is recommended only if Options 1 through 4 are operationally infeasible for this shift window and must not become a recurring practice.
      Preserves human constraints: No | Gap: ~0.0%

